Implemented Vercel Speed Insights integration

I successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js project following the latest official documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` package version compatible with the project
- Updated `package.json` and `package-lock.json` with the new dependency

### 2. Component Integration (app/components/RootShell.tsx)
- Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next";`
- Integrated the `<SpeedInsights />` component in the HTML body, positioned alongside the existing `<Analytics />` component
- The component was added to the shared RootShell component, which serves both English and Arabic root layouts, ensuring Speed Insights coverage across all pages

### 3. Test Updates (ops/portability.test.mjs)
- Updated the portability test to reflect the addition of `@vercel/speed-insights` as a second Vercel dependency
- Modified test assertion to expect both `@vercel/analytics` and `@vercel/speed-insights`
- Updated test message to clarify both dependencies degrade gracefully when running off-platform

## Implementation Details

The integration follows the framework-specific Next.js instructions from the official documentation:
- Used the Next.js-specific import path: `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Placed the component at the root level to track performance across all routes
- Component is positioned after children content and alongside Analytics, following best practices

## Verification

All verification steps completed successfully:
- ✅ Build completed without errors (npm run build)
- ✅ No new linting errors introduced
- ✅ All test suites pass (npm test)
- ✅ Package dependencies properly updated with lockfile

The Speed Insights component will begin collecting performance metrics once the application is deployed to Vercel and Speed Insights is enabled in the Vercel dashboard.

